    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 012058\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n158 PM PDT Tue Sept 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Drier, cooler, and breezy conditions expected through the work \n   week.  \n\n*  Uncertainty increases this weekend with regards to tropical \n   moisture from Marie. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Monday.\nMuch drier air has been working through the CWA over the past 24-\nhours as the eastern Pacific trough moves inland. Dew points in the \nLas Vegas Valley this morning were in the 60s, but have since \ndropped into the 40s or less. Other than a few flat cumulus, we are \nnot expected any showers or thunderstorms west of the Arizona and \nUtah borders. Moisture does remain over Mohave County where PWATs \nare currently ranging from 1.00-1.50\". We have already seen a few \nthunderstorms this morning over the far northern portions of county \nand with modest instability we should continue to see isolated \nthunderstorms through the remainder of this afternoon. PWATs really \ndrop Wednesday and Thursday as the drier air overspreads the entire \nforecast area leading to generally sunny skies across the forecast \narea. With the trough, we will seeing increasing southwest winds \nalong with daily afternoon breezes. Most areas will see winds \ngenerally 20-30 mph, but a few locations across Lincoln, Clark, \nMohave, and eastern San Bernardino counties will see a few gusts \naround 30-40 mph, although not widespread enough for wind \nadvisories. Temperatures over the next few days will also be a few \ndegrees below normal with Las Vegas likely not reaching 100 degrees \nat least through the end of the week. \n\nForecast confidence decreases heading into the weekend as we deal \nwith the potential moisture increase from newly formed Tropical \nStorm Marie. Both the EC and GFS ensemble members track the center \nof Marie north and eventually northwest by next weekend. The main \nquestion will be how much tropical moisture will be pulled into the \nDesert Southwest due to the trough off the west coast. At this \npoint, the models are indicating moisture increasing next weekend \nwith most areas south of I-15 seeing PWATs ranging from 1.50-2.00\". \nCurrently, NBM PoPs are generally 20-50% for most areas for next \nSunday and Monday, but that can certainly change due to the overall \nuncertainty due to the final track of Marie. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nBreezy conditions expected today. Southerly winds with gusts 20-30 \nkts through sunset. Winds will diminish but remain elevated at 10-15 \nkts for the overnight hours. Lower confidence if gusts persist \novernight, but for now keeping winds around 12 kts after 03z tonight \nin the TAF. Southerly winds will redevelop by mid morning on \nWednesday with gusts again of 20-30 kts. Temps remain below 100F \nthrough the forecast period. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Breezy southerly to \nwesterly winds expected into the evening with sustained winds at 10-\n20 kts and gusts of 20-30 kts. Confidence is higher that winds may \nremain elevated after sunset tonight at most sites, through at least \nmidnight, so keeping higher winds in the forecast for tonight than a \ntypical diurnal pattern. No cloud or vis issues expected.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow \nAVIATION...Czyzyk\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
